From: slaren <slarengh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dp: exit the PHY of an external DP over system
 suspend

With a DisplayPort sink connected, the external display stays dark after
every system suspend, and AUX transfers time out until the sink is
unplugged:

  [drm:drm_dp_dpcd_access] dpu_dp_aux: Too many retries, giving up. First error: -110
  [drm:msm_dp_bridge_detect [msm]] failed to read caps

While a sink is plugged in, the PHY stays initialised and the runtime PM
reference taken by msm_dp_hpd_plug_handle() keeps the controller active.
System suspend runtime suspends the controller anyway, through
pm_runtime_force_suspend(), which gates its clocks, and resuming it
resets the controller (msm_dp_display_host_init()). The PHY then has to
be initialised again, which the runtime PM callbacks only do for eDP.
For DP phy_initialized is still set, so the next detect or plug does not
initialise the PHY either, and every AUX transfer times out. Each of
them holds plugged_lock for seconds, which also stalls the compositor's
connector probing. The sink only comes back when an unplug
happens to exit the PHY.

The system suspend callback used to exit the PHY for every connector
type, before it deinitialised the controller; this got lost when the
driver switched to pm_runtime_force_suspend() and
pm_runtime_force_resume() for system sleep.

Exit the PHY of an external DP again before the controller is runtime
suspended for system sleep, provided no link is up: the next detect,
plug or modeset initialises it again, as after an unplug.

This does not depend on the system actually sleeping: with
/sys/power/pm_test set to "platform", where all device callbacks run but
the system does not enter suspend-to-idle, the sink is lost the same way
without this change and survives with it.

Tested on a Microsoft Surface Laptop 7 (x1e80100) with a USB-C
DisplayPort monitor behind the PS8830 retimer, in both cable
orientations, with the monitor unplugged, plugged in and switched off
while the system was suspended.

Fixes: 5814b8bf086a ("drm/msm/dp: incorporate pm_runtime framework into DP driver")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dp/dp_display.c
@@ -1310,10 +1310,35 @@ static int msm_dp_pm_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static int msm_dp_pm_suspend(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct msm_dp_display_private *dp = dev_get_dp_display_private(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * While a DP sink is plugged in, the PHY stays initialised and the
+	 * runtime PM reference taken by the plug handler keeps the controller
+	 * active. pm_runtime_force_suspend() runtime suspends it anyway, which
+	 * gates its clocks, and resuming it resets the controller, after which
+	 * the PHY has to be initialised again. The runtime PM callbacks only do
+	 * that for eDP, so exit the PHY here for DP as well: the next detect,
+	 * plug or modeset initialises it again.
+	 */
+	if (!dp->msm_dp_display.is_edp) {
+		guard(mutex)(&dp->plugged_lock);
+
+		if (dp->phy_initialized && !dp->msm_dp_display.power_on &&
+		    !pm_runtime_resume_and_get(dev)) {
+			msm_dp_display_host_phy_exit(dp);
+			pm_runtime_put_noidle(dev);
+		}
+	}
+
+	return pm_runtime_force_suspend(dev);
+}
+
 static const struct dev_pm_ops msm_dp_pm_ops = {
 	SET_RUNTIME_PM_OPS(msm_dp_pm_runtime_suspend, msm_dp_pm_runtime_resume, NULL)
-	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(pm_runtime_force_suspend,
-				pm_runtime_force_resume)
+	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(msm_dp_pm_suspend, pm_runtime_force_resume)
 };
 
 static struct platform_driver msm_dp_display_driver = {
